<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lotan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Litan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lotan is a seven-headed sea serpent in the Ugaritic texts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, called the fleeing serpent and the twisting serpent, and defeated by the storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> and the goddess Anat. A servant of the sea god Yam, it embodies the forces of chaos that the storm god overcomes. The same epithets are applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leviathan">Leviathan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> 27:1, showing the debt of biblical poetry to Canaanite myth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lotan.jpg" alt="Lotan" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Doré · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Destruction_of_Leviathan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
